# Carried over from ../Website/.env. Most are renamed EXPO_PUBLIC_* so Expo
# inlines them; the Square ids deliberately are not — see their note below.
#
# Everything here is public by design. The Firebase web apiKey is not a secret
# (docs/integrations.md says so outright) - firestore.rules is the protection.
# The Square *access token* is a real secret and is NOT here: it lives in Secret
# Manager and never leaves the Cloud Function.
#
# NOTE: React Native Firebase does NOT read these for its own init. It reads
# google-services.json / GoogleService-Info.plist. These exist for the things
# that talk to Firebase over plain REST (the theme document) and for Square.

EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=

# Read by src/firebase/appCheck.ts. App Attest and Play Integrity both refuse a
# simulator or an unsigned build, so local work needs a debug token registered
# in the Firebase console - see ../Website/docs/gallery-console-setup.md 1d.
# Leave it empty for a real device build; __DEV__ is what selects the provider.
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APPCHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN=
EXPO_PUBLIC_AZURE_TENANT_ID=

# Square. Unprefixed on purpose, and both environments live here at once.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* is inlined into the JS bundle by literal textual match, so
# prefixing these would ship BOTH sets of ids inside the binary and reduce the
# choice between them to a runtime branch — which a bug or a stale toggle can
# flip in a released build, and the failure mode is charging real cards while
# the screen still says test mode. app.config.ts resolves one pair at build
# time and only that pair reaches the app, which is what makes holding both
# here safe.
#
# Neither id is a secret: both are visible in the network tab of any site using
# Square's SDK and authorize nothing alone. SQUARE_ACCESS_TOKEN is the real
# credential, it lives in Secret Manager, and it is deliberately absent here.
#
# All four are in ../Website/.env already — sandbox under SQUARE_SANDBOX_*,
# production under NEXT_PUBLIC_SQUARE_*.
#
# Sandbox is the default and production is opt-in, once, by hand:
#   SQUARE_ENV=production npx expo run:ios
# A release build must set SQUARE_ENV=production in its build environment.
SQUARE_SANDBOX_APPLICATION_ID=
SQUARE_SANDBOX_LOCATION_ID=
SQUARE_APPLICATION_ID=
SQUARE_LOCATION_ID=

# Apple Pay's merchant id, on team 5ZRM8X66TN. Leave blank until it exists and
# Square holds the matching payment-processing certificate: app.config.ts omits
# the entitlement while this is empty, because an entitlement naming a merchant
# id that does not exist fails code signing rather than failing at runtime.
APPLE_PAY_MERCHANT_ID=

EXPO_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN=https://spsm-database.web.app

# The church's Tithe.ly giving form.
#
# Both optional, and both blank here on purpose: the form id is committed in
# src/giving/config.ts for the reason the website commits its own copy — a
# giving page that depends on an env var is a giving page that is broken in
# every build that is not a laptop with a .env, which is what happened on the
# website for eight months. These override it, so a test form can be put in
# front of the Give button without a code change.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_TITHELY_FORM_ID is a form id; EXPO_PUBLIC_TITHELY_GIVING_URL is a
# whole address, for the day the church's giving page is not a Tithe.ly form at
# all.
EXPO_PUBLIC_TITHELY_FORM_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_TITHELY_GIVING_URL=
